an": 1.0, "environment": 8.3, "incumbency": 2.0, "money": 0.93}, "rating_margin": 13.9, "sigma_race": 6.5, "sigma_total": 7.12, "baseline_margin": 13.23, "analyst_adjustment": -0.73, "key_factors": ["Riley holds a massive cash edge: $2.2M on hand vs. $43K for Oberacker", "House Republicans pulled roughly $5M in planned outside spending from the seat", "Raters split between Safe/Solid D and Likely D; markets price Riley near 96%", "No public polling in a district decided by fewer than three points in each of the last two cycles"], "rationale": "Riley's fundraising dominance and the Republican withdrawal of outside spending point to a comfortable incumbent win, and raters and prediction markets broadly agree the seat is out of play. The district's history of single-digit margins and the absence of any public polling keep the expected margin from going higher.", "watch": "A late return of Republican outside spending or a credible poll showing Oberacker within single digits would warrant revisiting.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Josh Riley (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Rep. Josh Riley seeks a second term against Republican Peter Oberacker in a district that has been decided by fewer than three points in each of the last two elections. Riley holds an overwhelming financial edge, raising about $5.8 million to Oberacker's $1.4 million and entering the final weeks with roughly $2.2 million on hand against about $43,000. National Republican groups have pulled around $5 million in planned advertising from the race, a sign that outside strategists see it slipping from contention.\n\nNo public general-election polls are available, so expectations rest on fundamentals, expert ratings and markets. Rating outlets split between Safe/Solid D and Likely D, and prediction markets price Riley's chances near 96 percent. The combination of incumbency, money and a favorable national